You're right: the solution as it stands only works for wires close to the current height, and I have now measured how quickly it degrades. The wire's centreline is 10 cm by default, and I raised it for all four legs (seeds 0–3 each):

Wire raise	Wire top	Result
+1 cm	about 11.6 cm	mostly passes (earlier test)
+2 cm	about 12.6 cm	FL 3/4, FR 1/4, RL 3/3, RR 3/4
+4 cm	about 14.6 cm	0 passes; many runs never touch the wire
+6 cm	about 16.6 cm	0 passes; the calf never touches the wire at all
Why it fails, in two parts:

Detection: the recovery only starts when the calf touches the wire. A higher wire hits the thigh or body instead, so the recovery never starts, however good the step is. In the +6 cm runs the front-leg robots stop at the wire (base x ≈ 0 for the whole run) and the rear-leg robots just walk on. I didn't investigate why, and note that this test only lets the target leg interact with the wire, so high-wire results are not realistic.
Reach: the step lifts the foot a fixed 20 cm command above where it starts, but the foot only reaches about 14–16 cm above its start. Calf clearance in passing runs is about 8–12 cm above a 10.6 cm wire. The leg's geometry also caps the height: the hip is about 30 cm high and the foot can't come closer than about 11–13 cm to it, so the foot tops out near 18–20 cm off the ground. My rough estimate is that a wire above about 15 cm can't be stepped over by this leg alone. The robot would need a different strategy, such as lowering the body, jumping or going around. That is my estimate, not something I've tested.
Adapting the scripted approach. This is cheap and I'd try it first:

Make the lift depend on the wire height: lift = wire height + margin, not a fixed 20 cm. The leg's own joint angles at contact already tell us roughly how high the wire is.
Start recovery on contact with the thigh and body as well as the calf.
Re-tune the start delays per wire height, since I tuned them at one height.
This would probably extend the range up to the geometric limit, maybe a wire of 13–15 cm. It would not go beyond it.

Can training help? Yes, but for variety, not for beating the geometry:

What it's good for: a policy trained with randomized wire heights, positions, speeds and approach angles could learn when to start and how high to lift from what it senses, instead of fixed numbers I tuned by hand. That would remove my open-loop timers.
What it can't do: it can't lift a foot higher than the leg can physically reach.
Our history: the earlier learned attempt failed because the walking policy overrode its corrections.
A safer way: keep the scripted step as the base. Let the learned part pick only a few numbers, such as lift height, advance and start time, from what the robot senses. Start it from the successful scripted runs, and score it with the same strict pass rule. I'd also randomize the wire height widely during training.
Risks: a learned policy can find loopholes. We already saw one, a robot that fell sideways past the wire counted as a success. And everything is simulation only.
Should I first try the cheap fix, scaling the lift with wire height and adding thigh contact? I'd rather learn how far that goes before deciding whether training is worth the cost.
